          "tz": -360
        },
        "committer": {
          "name": "Charlie Olmstead",
          "email": "charlie@whamcloud.com",
          "date": "2026-10-12 17:48:49.000000000",
          "tz": -360
        },
        "subject": "LU-20539 changed.py: rewrite subtest change detection",
        "message": "LU-20539 changed.py: rewrite subtest change detection\n\nRewrite changed.py to analyze the commit from the repository instead of\nparsing the output of git format-patch. The script now takes\n\"[-C repo_dir] [rev]\" (default: cwd, FETCH_HEAD), reads the pre- and\npost-image suite files with git show and locates the changes and\naffected paths with git diff-tree.\n\nA subtest spans from its column 0 test_N() definition to its column 0\n\"run_test N\" line. Removed lines are attributed by the pre-image ranges\nand added lines by the post-image ranges; lines outside any range\n(suite setup, helpers defined between tests, test_* functions with no\nrun_test) are not attributed to a subtest. A subtest is only reported\nwhen the post-image still has its run_test line.\n\nProblems with the patch-only parser that this fixes:\n\n- Duplicate subtests. The \"+run_test N\" path appended to updated_tests\n  without checking for an existing entry, so a test whose body changed\n  and whose run_test line was also in the diff was listed twice. Tests\n  are now collected in a set.\n\n- Wrong function attribution. The enclosing function was taken from\n  the \"@@ ... @@ context\" hunk header, which git fills with the\n  nearest preceding column-0 word: often a helper, a run_test line, a\n  variable assignment or the previous test. Changes were attributed\n  to whichever name happened to be there, or dropped. Every changed\n  line is now matched to the test range that contains it.\n\n- Context-line tracking only worked when a \"test_N()\" line fell inside\n  the three context lines of a hunk, so changes deep in a function\n  body were attributed to the hunk-header name instead.\n\n- Removed and renamed tests were reported. A \"-run_test N\" line or a\n  deleted body produced test_N even though the post-image no longer\n  runs it, and the session then failed to find the subtest. When two\n  tests shared a name and one was renamed, the old shared name was\n  reported as well. The post-image run_test filter drops these.\n\n- test_* helpers that are not subtests (test_dom in sanity-quota,\n  pjdfstest's test_()) were reported because they start with\n  \"test_\". They have no run_test line so they are no longer attributed\n  or reported.\n\n- Changing only a run_test description, or only the test_N()\n  definition line, was reported as a subtest change. Those lines no\n  longer count as changes; new tests are still detected through their\n  new run_test line.\n\n- Added IGNORED_TESTS (test_sanity, test_sanityn) for these wrapper\n  suite files which fail to run properly with ONLY_REPEAT\n\naffected_files is now taken from git diff-tree --no-renames\n--name-status rather than parsed from \"diff --git\" headers.\n\nPreserved: blank and comment-only changes are ignored, the\nparallel-scale-nfs to nfsv3/nfsv4 expansion, the SkipTesting path\nrules, only lustre/tests/*.sh and lustre/tests/runtests are analyzed\nas suites\n\nSigned-off-by: Charlie Olmstead <charlie@whamcloud.com>\nChange-Id: Id93b1b3c2fc1695a48b2d258e8697d1ad287e3d4\n"
      },
      "branch": "refs/heads/master"
    }
